<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rama II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1767">1767</a>–<a href="/years/1824">1824</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Buddha Loetla Nabhalai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> II, Phra Phutthaloetla Naphalai, was the second king of the Chakri dynasty of Siam, reigning from 1809 to 1824. A son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a>, he presided over a period of peace and revived trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, while the court became a centre of the arts. He was himself a leading poet, the author of a version of the Inao dance-drama, and patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunthorn-phu">Sunthorn Phu</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rama-ii.jpg" alt="Rama II" width="320" height="488" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Rama_II_of_Siam_(Naphalai)_Portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wet-rice farming in the Chao Phraya basin fed the population, while the court profited from royal trade monopolies and from the junk trade with China, which flourished under <b>Rama II</b> and Rama III and was largely run by Chinese merchants.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>A group of Siamese traditional musicians chose &quot;Bulan Loi Luean&quot;, a composition of King <b>Rama II</b>, and Chulalongkorn ordered Christopher Hewetson, a Dutch bandmaster in the Royal Siamese Army, to arrange it in western style for military band.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sansoen-phra-barami">Sansoen Phra Barami</a></li></ul></section>
